WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:08.200
Microneedle patches usually sit on plastic that sweat cannot pass. When sweat pools, delivering drugs and sensing sweat on one patch becomes hard.

2
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:19.160
The team used Korean mulberry paper, whose fibers leave connected pores. A water droplet stayed put on plastic but soaked into the paper within six seconds.

3
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:28.440
On top, needles 1 millimeter tall were 3D printed with light cured resin, and they bonded to the paper more strongly than to plastic.

4
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:40.760
Carving the needle sides raised the dye loading, relative to needle mass, from 12.6 to 25.36 percent, about double, tested with a fluorescent dye in place of a drug.

5
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:49.080
Electrodes printed on the paper detected uric acid, a gout marker, down to 9.3 nanomolar.

6
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:56.760
Drug delivery and sweat sensing on one breathable sheet of paper. Testing both together is the next step.
